<Base
  title="About — Stephen “Stevie” Thomas"
  description="Hitting Trainer at Driveline Baseball in Tampa, working on swing mechanics, biomechanics, and public baseball data."
>
  <section style="padding: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem) 0 0;">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal" style="--i:0">About</p>
    <h1 class="reveal" style="--i:1">Stephen “Stevie” Thomas</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm a hitting trainer at Driveline Baseball in Tampa, Florida. I work with hitters from
        youth ball through the professional level — assessments, programming, cage work, and the
        long unglamorous middle where a change actually sticks.
      </p>

      <p>
        I came to this out of biomedical engineering, not baseball. I finished a B.S. at The
        Catholic University of America in 2025, where I played four years of varsity baseball and
        picked up All-Landmark and All-Region honors, then spent a season at UConn before moving
        into coaching full time. The engineering side is why I keep pulling on the measurement
        question: the gap between what a hitter feels and what actually happened. A swing can feel
        identical twice and produce a barrel and a whiff. Bat tracking, force plates, and markerless
        motion capture have made that gap measurable in a way it wasn't ten years ago.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most of my outside-the-cage time goes into using that well — which usually means figuring
        out which numbers are load-bearing and which ones are just along for the ride. I build most
        of my own tools. Nothing here is a product; it's a coach with a Python install and a
        stubborn question.
      </p>

      <h3>What I'll talk about all day</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Bat path, attack angle, and why "swing up" is an incomplete instruction</li>
        <li>Variability in the swing as a skill rather than a defect</li>
        <li>Constraint-led drill design and what actually transfers to a game</li>
        <li>The run-value math that quietly decides how a lineup should be built</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The fine print</h3>
      <p>
        Everything on this site is either built on public data or described at the level of method.
        Athlete data, client work, and anything internal to Driveline stays off it. If a project
        page reads a little abstract, that's why.
      </p>

      <h3>Get in touch</h3>
      <p>
        Easiest is <a href="mailto:mesteviet@gmail.com">mesteviet@gmail.com</a>, or
        <a href="https://x.com/stevie_t12">@stevie_t12</a> on X. I'm happy to talk shop with coaches
        and players, and I'd rather be told I'm wrong than agreed with politely.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
